WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.879
This lesson gets easier once the learner stops treating equations, graphs, and checkpoints as separate topics.

2
00:00:06.879 --> 00:00:13.758
In ideal SHM, one displacement equation can reveal amplitude, angular frequency, ordinary frequency, period, and even hints about speed.

3
00:00:13.758 --> 00:00:20.637
The time graphs are not separate chapters.

4
00:00:20.637 --> 00:00:27.516
They are pictures of the same motion.

5
00:00:27.516 --> 00:00:34.395
Turning points, equilibrium crossings, and quarter-cycle shifts then become checkpoints that explain which quantity is largest at a given instant.

6
00:00:34.395 --> 00:00:41.275
Once those links are secure, the worked example feels like reading a well-organized system rather than collecting unrelated formulas.

7
00:00:41.275 --> 00:00:48.154
Keep this takeaway in view: The lesson opens cleanly once equations, traces, and checkpoints are read as one SHM story.

8
00:00:48.154 --> 00:00:55.033
Equation means compressed story.

9
00:00:55.033 --> 00:01:01.912
The coefficients in x equals A cos omega t already encode the size and timing of the oscillation.

10
00:01:01.912 --> 00:01:08.791
Graphs means same motion, new view.

11
00:01:08.791 --> 00:01:15.670
Displacement, velocity, and acceleration traces are linked views of one oscillator.

12
00:01:15.670 --> 00:01:23.231
This diagram matters because it shows that the graph and the equation are not rivals.

13
00:01:23.231 --> 00:01:30.792
The amplitude in the equation matches the vertical reach of the displacement trace.

14
00:01:30.792 --> 00:01:38.354
The timing of one full repeat gives the period, while the number of repeats per second gives the frequency.

15
00:01:38.354 --> 00:01:45.915
The linked traces then show that velocity and acceleration do not peak at the same instants as displacement.

16
00:01:45.915 --> 00:01:53.476
That is the exact kind of connected reading the worked example demands.

17
00:01:53.476 --> 00:02:01.038
Keep this takeaway in view: The graph board works because it turns equation reading and trace reading into one connected habit.

18
00:02:01.038 --> 00:02:08.599
Amplitude: The coefficient in the displacement equation matches the maximum displacement on the trace.

19
00:02:08.599 --> 00:02:16.160
Timing: One full repeat on the time axis gives the period and therefore the frequency.

20
00:02:16.160 --> 00:02:21.403
A useful analogy is one dancer on a rehearsal floor being watched by three systems at once.

21
00:02:21.403 --> 00:02:26.647
A position camera says where the dancer is.

22
00:02:26.647 --> 00:02:31.890
A speed meter says how fast the dancer is moving.

23
00:02:31.890 --> 00:02:37.133
A pull meter says how strongly the return pull is acting.

24
00:02:37.133 --> 00:02:42.377
The dancer is one person performing one motion, yet the three displays peak at different moments.

25
00:02:42.377 --> 00:02:47.620
SHM works the same way.

26
00:02:47.620 --> 00:02:52.863
The traces differ because they measure different quantities, not because different motions are happening.

27
00:02:52.863 --> 00:02:58.107
Keep this takeaway in view: The dancer analogy helps because it makes phase-shifted displays feel natural without splitting the motion into separate stories.

28
00:02:58.107 --> 00:03:03.350
One performer means one oscillator.

29
00:03:03.350 --> 00:03:08.593
The system is the same even though the displays emphasize different quantities.

30
00:03:08.593 --> 00:03:13.837
Different displays means different peaks.

31
00:03:13.837 --> 00:03:19.080
Position, speed, and restoring response reach their largest values at different checkpoints.

32
00:03:19.080 --> 00:03:26.038
The key formula set starts with x equals A cos omega t or x equals A sin omega t.

33
00:03:26.038 --> 00:03:32.995
The coefficient A gives the amplitude.

34
00:03:32.995 --> 00:03:39.952
The omega term gives the angular frequency.

35
00:03:39.952 --> 00:03:46.910
From that, ordinary frequency follows from omega over two pi, and the period follows from one over frequency.

36
00:03:46.910 --> 00:03:53.867
Maximum speed in SHM then comes from omega A.

37
00:03:53.867 --> 00:04:00.825
These relations matter because they let the learner pull several answers from one compact expression without memorizing disconnected steps.

38
00:04:00.825 --> 00:04:07.782
Keep this takeaway in view: The formula scene works because it turns one SHM equation into a reliable map of size, timing, and maximum speed.

39
00:04:07.782 --> 00:04:14.740
Do not skip omega: Angular frequency is the hinge that connects the equation to ordinary frequency and period.

40
00:04:14.740 --> 00:04:21.698
One equation, many answers: Amplitude, frequency, period, and maximum speed can all come from the same displacement expression.

41
00:04:21.698 --> 00:04:28.655
x = A cos(omega t) or x = A sin(omega t).

42
00:04:28.655 --> 00:04:35.612
f = omega / 2pi and T = 1/f.

43
00:04:35.612 --> 00:04:42.570
A strong solution reads A and omega first, then converts timing quantities in a clean order.

44
00:04:42.570 --> 00:04:50.161
A strong routine is to read the amplitude and angular frequency first, convert omega into frequency and period second, and only then use a chosen time to inspect the phase checkpoint.

45
00:04:50.161 --> 00:04:57.752
This order matters because learners often jump into calculator work before they have even named the constants.

46
00:04:57.752 --> 00:05:05.344
The safer route is read the constants, convert the timing, then inspect what the phase says about displacement, speed, or acceleration at a selected instant.

47
00:05:05.344 --> 00:05:12.935
Keep this takeaway in view: Read-convert-checkpoint is the safest SHM-equation routine because it separates constant reading from phase interpretation.

48
00:05:12.935 --> 00:05:20.526
Step 1 means read constants.

49
00:05:20.526 --> 00:05:28.118
Pull A and omega from the equation before doing any time substitution.

50
00:05:28.118 --> 00:05:35.709
Step 2 means convert timing.

51
00:05:35.709 --> 00:05:43.300
Use omega to get frequency and period in a clean, explicit order.

52
00:05:43.300 --> 00:05:50.007
The phase map matters because it tells the learner what each landmark means.

53
00:05:50.007 --> 00:05:56.713
At a turning point, displacement is maximum, speed is zero, and the restoring acceleration magnitude is maximum inward.

54
00:05:56.713 --> 00:06:03.420
At an equilibrium crossing, displacement is zero, speed is maximum, and acceleration is zero.

55
00:06:03.420 --> 00:06:10.127
Those checkpoints repeat every quarter cycle.

56
00:06:10.127 --> 00:06:16.833
Once that rhythm is understood, a time value is no longer just a number.

57
00:06:16.833 --> 00:06:23.540
It becomes a clue about the motion state.

58
00:06:23.540 --> 00:06:30.247
Keep this takeaway in view: Quarter-cycle checkpoints let the learner classify SHM motion states quickly without inventing a new rule each time.

59
00:06:30.247 --> 00:06:36.953
Turning point: Maximum displacement means zero speed and maximum restoring pull magnitude.

60
00:06:36.953 --> 00:06:43.660
Equilibrium crossing: Zero displacement means maximum speed and zero restoring acceleration.

61
00:06:43.660 --> 00:06:47.409
Try four checks.

62
00:06:47.409 --> 00:06:51.159
Does the amplitude come from the coefficient of the displacement equation?

63
00:06:51.159 --> 00:06:54.908
Yes.

64
00:06:54.908 --> 00:06:58.657
Can omega be converted into ordinary frequency?

65
00:06:58.657 --> 00:07:02.407
Yes.

66
00:07:02.407 --> 00:07:06.156
Is speed maximum at a turning point?

67
00:07:06.156 --> 00:07:09.905
No, it is maximum when the oscillator crosses equilibrium.

68
00:07:09.905 --> 00:07:13.655
And can one SHM equation support both timing answers and motion-state answers?

69
00:07:13.655 --> 00:07:17.404
Yes.

70
00:07:17.404 --> 00:07:21.153
Those checks show whether the learner is truly reading one connected motion story.

71
00:07:21.153 --> 00:07:24.903
Keep this takeaway in view: The worked example is ready once the learner can move smoothly from constants, to timing, to motion state.

72
00:07:24.903 --> 00:07:28.652
Coefficient means amplitude.

73
00:07:28.652 --> 00:07:32.401
Read A before doing anything else with the equation.

74
00:07:32.401 --> 00:07:36.151
Omega means timing hinge.

75
00:07:36.151 --> 00:07:39.900
Use omega to obtain both frequency and period cleanly.

76
00:07:39.900 --> 00:07:46.905
You are ready for the worked example now.

77
00:07:46.905 --> 00:07:53.909
Start by reading the amplitude and angular frequency directly from the displacement equation.

78
00:07:53.909 --> 00:08:00.914
Convert omega into ordinary frequency and period.

79
00:08:00.914 --> 00:08:07.918
Then, if the question asks about a chosen time, use the phase to decide where the oscillator is in the cycle and what that says about the speed.

80
00:08:07.918 --> 00:08:14.923
The strongest answers in this lesson sound like one connected reading of the same motion from several angles.

81
00:08:14.923 --> 00:08:21.927
Keep this takeaway in view: The SHM graph-and-equation worked example becomes much safer once the learner reads constants and checkpoints in a fixed order.

82
00:08:21.927 --> 00:08:28.932
Avoid formula collecting: If the learner gathers equations without classifying the phase, the specific-time part usually becomes weaker.

83
00:08:28.932 --> 00:08:35.936
Use words with the math: A sentence about turning points or equilibrium crossings often proves the phase reasoning was real.

84
00:08:35.936 --> 00:08:42.941
read A and omega first.

85
00:08:42.941 --> 00:08:49.945
convert omega into frequency and period before substituting times.

86
00:08:49.945 --> 00:08:56.950
A strong solution keeps the equation, trace logic, and phase checkpoints aligned all the way through.
